==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|targe|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|targa|lang=ang}} (masculine) and {{term|targe|lang=ang}} (feminine), both from {{etyl|non|en}} {{term|targa||round shield|lang=non}} from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*targǭ||edge}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*dArg'h-||fenced lot}}. Akin to {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|zarga||side wall, rim|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Zarge||border, frame|lang=de}}). Reinforced in Middle English by Old French {{term|targe|lang=fro}} (French {{term|targe|lang=fr}}), from the same Germanic source.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/tɑːd͡ʒ/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/tɑɹd͡ʒ/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|archaic|lang=en}} A small [[shield]].
#* '''1819''', The Jester wore his usual fantastic habit, but late accidents had led him to adopt a good cutting falchion, instead of his wooden sword, with a '''targe''' to match it — Walter Scott, ''Ivanhoe''

